WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.460
Section three-fourteen point four, paragraph c, requires safeguards tied to identified risks.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.480
Begin with access control.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:19.180
Authenticate authorized users and limit each person to the customer information needed for assigned duties, while customers receive access only to their own information.

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.000
Review those permissions periodically.

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:34.260
Technical controls, physical restrictions, joiner and leaver processes, and privileged-access governance should operate as one system rather than as disconnected checklists.

6
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:45.900
The institution must identify and manage the data, personnel, devices, systems, and facilities supporting business purposes according to their importance and the risk strategy.

7
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.320
This is more than an asset spreadsheet.

8
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:58.420
Effective inventories connect customer-information flows, owners, locations, dependencies, and protective controls so change and incident decisions use reliable scope.

9
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.780
Customer information must be encrypted at rest and while transmitted over external networks.

10
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:12.880
If encryption is infeasible, effective alternative compensating controls may be used only with review and approval by the Qualified Individual.

11
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:20.420
The decision should document scope, technical reason, residual risk, protective alternatives, and conditions for reconsideration.

12
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.760
Applications developed internally need secure development practices.

13
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:34.540
Externally developed applications used to transmit, access, or store customer information need procedures for security evaluation, assessment, or testing.

14
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.540
Multi-factor authentication is required for individuals accessing information systems.

15
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:45.480
A reasonably equivalent or more secure access control may be used when the Qualified Individual approves it in writing.

16
00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:56.220
Authentication design should account for administrators, remote users, service providers, recovery paths, and systems that indirectly expose customer information.

17
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:06.340
The Rule also requires procedures for secure disposal no later than two years after the last use of customer information in providing a product or service.

18
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:18.760
Exceptions include legitimate business or operational needs, another legal retention requirement, or situations where targeted disposal is not reasonably feasible because of how information is maintained.

19
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.660
Retention policies must be reviewed periodically to minimize unnecessary data.

20
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:34.100
Finally, adopt change-management procedures and controls that monitor and log authorized-user activity while detecting unauthorized access, use, or tampering.

21
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:49.400
Evidence matters: approvals, access reviews, encryption coverage, application assessments, disposal records, change records, logs, alerts, investigations, and remediation should show that safeguards operate.

22
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:57.180
Documentation cannot replace control performance, but unsupported control claims cannot demonstrate a functioning program.
